// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT OR Apache-2.0
// Regression for [M-cancel-loop-accept-swallowed-residual] (221.1 bug-sweep
// #15, 2026-07-23 — residual of the already-merged [M-nv-cancel-loop-accept-
// swallowed] listener-refcount fix). Root cause, confirmed by direct
// instrumentation of `nova_fiber_yield`/`nova_scope_deliver_cancel`/sysmon
// (compiler-codegen/nova_rt/fibers.h + runtime.c):
//
// Under armed M:N, the WORKER's own fiber-resume preamble repoints the TLS
// `_nova_active_scope` to the worker's bookkeeping scope (`&w->scope`) for
// the ENTIRE life of a worker-run fiber — see `nova_runtime_cancel_worker_
// fibers`'s doc comment in runtime.c. `nova_scope_deliver_cancel` (fired by
// `supervised(timeout:/deadline:)` on expiry) sets `cancel_requested` on the
// USER-LEVEL supervised scope — a genuinely DIFFERENT `NovaFiberQueue` object
// than `&w->scope`. `nova_fiber_yield`'s cooperative-cancellation check used
// to read ONLY `_nova_active_scope->cancel_requested` — for a worker-run
// fiber that never parks (a busy/CPU-bound loop with no blocking op at all,
// or one whose every op returns an immediate non-parking `Err` post-cancel —
// exactly a `while`-retry-loop's shape once the underlying resource's stage
// has already flipped to CLOSING), this NEVER observed the real cancellation
// — confirmed empirically: `nova_preempt_check` WAS correctly firing
// `nova_fiber_yield` every ~10ms sysmon slice (preemption itself works
// fine), but the cancel-check inside it was reading the WRONG scope's flag,
// so the loop spun until the outer test/process timeout, not the
// `supervised(timeout:)` deadline. Only genuinely-PARKED ops (net.c
// read/write/accept, `Time.sleep`) were ever cancelled correctly — via the
// entirely separate stop_cb fan-out in `nova_scope_deliver_cancel`/
// `nova_runtime_cancel_worker_fibers`, which never touches
// `_nova_active_scope` at all.
//
// Fix (`nova_fiber_yield`, fibers.h): when `_nova_active_scope` itself isn't
// flagged, fall back to the fiber's OWN ctx (`NovaSpawnCtxBase.
// _nova_parent_scope`, set exactly ONCE at spawn time to the REAL user-level
// scope the fiber was spawned into, and never repointed afterward unlike
// `_nova_active_scope`) and check ITS `cancel_requested` too.
//
// NARROWER-THAN-THE-FULL-SYMPTOM NOTE (found investigating this fixture):
// wrapping the busy-loop in a `consume x = ...` binding with D432
// auto-`@cleanup` (e.g. an accept-retry-loop over a `consume lst
// TcpListener`) reintroduces an UNCANCELLABLE shape even with this fix —
// `nv_consume_enter_shield`/`leave_shield` (Plan 110.2.1, D188 R3) is armed
// for the WHOLE consume-scope body (not just the actual `@cleanup` dispatch,
// per emit_c.rs's own "cancel-shield over body + cleanup" comment), which
// makes `nova_fiber_yield`'s cancel-throw check permanently deferred
// (`cancel_mask_count > 0`) for as long as ANY `consume`-bound resource with
// auto-cleanup is alive in the fiber — confirmed via the SAME
// instrumentation (`mask=1` throughout the loop). That is DELIBERATE,
// documented design (not touched by this fix) and a materially bigger,
// separate architectural question (every consume-scope in the codebase),
// not a narrow net.c/scheduler bug — left as a follow-up decision for the
// owner rather than silently changed here. This fixture therefore covers
// ONLY the general busy-loop/scope-mismatch class (no consume-scope
// involved), which the fix above fully resolves; the accept-loop-specific
// symptom (`m222_7_cancel_loop_accept_swallowed.nv`, unbounded `continue`
// variant) is a compound of BOTH factors and remains only partially fixed —
// see the 221.1 registry entry for the shield follow-up marker.
//
// EXPECT_TIMEOUT_MS 20000

module standalone.m2217_15_cancel_busyloop_scope_mismatch

import std.time.duration

test "supervised(timeout:) cancels a pure CPU-bound busy loop (no consume-scope shield) via cooperative preempt" {
    with Fail[TimeoutError] = |_e| {} {
        supervised(timeout: 300.to_millis()) {
            spawn {
                mut n = 0
                while true {
                    n = n + 1
                }
            }
        }
    }
    assert(true)
}
